What you should learn next
To turn this into a portfolio-grade, production-ready project:

Testing — there are no tests. Add pytest unit tests for the parsers (use small fixture logs) and httpx-based API tests. This is the single biggest gap.
Async correctness — the heavy parsing runs synchronously inside an async endpoint, blocking the event loop. Learn run_in_executor / background workers (Celery, RQ, or FastAPI BackgroundTasks + a queue).
Refactoring large modules — the two ~3,000-line files repeat a lot of logic. Learn to extract the shared parser/check framework into a common package (DRY, separation of concerns).
CI/CD — add GitHub Actions / GitLab CI to run lint + tests + Docker build on every push.
Cloud deployment — deploy it live (Render, Railway, Fly.io, or AWS ECS) so you can put a working URL on your resume.
Type safety & validation — Pydantic models for responses, error schemas, and a typed API contract.
Frontend polish — upload progress, error states, drag-and-drop, and maybe rendering the PDF inline.
Security — input fuzzing on the parser (malformed binaries can crash it), rate limiting, and auth if it ever goes public.